---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Battery Equalization: The Controlled Overcharge That Saves Flooded Batteries';
const description = 'Equalization is a deliberate, controlled overcharge of a flooded battery bank — it remixes stratified acid and balances the cells. When to equalize and why AGM and gel must never be.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/equalization/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Battery equalization',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is battery equalization?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A deliberate, controlled overcharge at a raised voltage (roughly 15-16V for a 12V flooded bank) that remixes stratified acid, balances the cells, and reverses early sulfation. It is standard maintenance for flooded deep-cycle banks.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can you equalize AGM or gel batteries?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'No. Equalization gasses a sealed battery past its recombination limit and vents electrolyte that cannot be replaced. It is a flooded-only procedure; sealed batteries must never be equalized.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Charging · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Battery Equalization: The Controlled Overcharge That Saves Flooded Batteries</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        There is exactly one situation where overcharging a battery is correct maintenance — and it is flooded-only,
        scheduled, and controlled. It is called equalization.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Equalization is a <strong>deliberate, controlled overcharge</strong> at a
        raised voltage (roughly <strong>15–16V for a 12V flooded bank</strong>, per manufacturer guidance) that
        <strong>remixes stratified acid, balances the cells and reverses early sulfation</strong>. It is
        <strong>flooded-only</strong> — AGM and gel must never be equalized, because gassing vents their sealed
        electrolyte permanently.
      </div>

      <h2>What it fixes</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>How equalization helps</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Acid stratification</strong></td><td>The gassing remixes the acid that sinks to the bottom</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Cell imbalance</strong></td><td>The raised voltage brings lagging cells to full</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Early sulfation</strong></td><td>The controlled overcharge softens young sulfate crystals</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The flooded-only rule</h2>
      <p>
        Equalization gasses deliberately — the bubbles are the mechanism. A flooded battery tolerates the gassing
        (you top up with <a href="/lead-acid/battery-water/">distilled water</a> afterward); a sealed
        <a href="https://technology.worldbatteryhub.com/vrla/">VRLA</a> battery vents that gas permanently through
        its valve, drying the mat or gel — the same damage as
        <a href="/lead-acid/agm-charging/">overcharging an AGM</a>, on purpose. Sealed designs balance by other
        means, not by equalization.
      </p>

      <h2>When and how</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Schedule it</strong> — monthly-to-quarterly for cycled flooded banks, per manufacturer guidance.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch it</strong> — voltage, temperature and specific gravity tell you when it is done.</li>
        <li><strong>Water after</strong> — the gassing costs water; top up with distilled.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>Position:</strong> Equalization is the exception that proves the overcharge rule — controlled and
          flooded-only, it extends deep-cycle banks; applied to sealed batteries, it destroys them — and the
          flooded/sealed distinction is the entire safety of the procedure.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Reasoning:</strong> The same gassing that balances a flooded bank vents a sealed one dry. The
          procedure's value and its danger come from the same physics, which is why the flooded-only rule is
          absolute — and why the chargers that offer equalization only offer it on flooded profiles.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from battery engineering references and charger/BCI guidance.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — equalization guidance.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer charging documentation — equalization voltage and schedule.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
